  "notes": "The lane's most explicitly 70s-arranged crossover voice, and the entry describes a lineage rather than a pattern: \"the lineage is a sampled 70s break re-played by a band\" — \"dry, tight, break-style kit recorded with minimal reverb — kick on 1 and the offbeat of 2, snare cracked on 2&4, tambourine doubling, congas and cowbell continuous, and single-stroke fills\". So `secondaryAnchor` is 2&, the percussion runs 4–7 hits a bar across congas, cowbell and tambourine, and the hat stays out of the way at a fill density of 0.2. Harmony is the two families the entry writes out — `i–iv–V` and the `i–VII–VI–V` descent — with dominant-7th blues colour, so triads and sevenths carry the spread and ninths nearly vanish. Melody is stated as chant: \"mid-baritone-to-tenor, plain-toned, short and highly repetitive phrases designed as chants, low melisma, with a group-vocal or choir answer\", which is the riff-loop structure, two-and-four-bar phrases, the highest `chordToneBias` in this batch and a dense answering counter at +12. Bass is \"flatwounds, syncopated 8ths and 16ths locked to the kick, mixed round and forward\" — locked, so `mirror_kick`, but at a root probability well below the section's pure root players because the line is syncopated. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody figures; the research marks the tempo and drum values as inference and [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The a cappella-plus-claps hook variant the entry calls a signature device is expressed only as the claps-and-voice breakdown in the arrangement.",
